# Public builds: signed, hashed, transparent (hard rule)

AIC is a cybersecurity company. We are meticulously transparent in our
processes and products. **Secure by design.** When agents **build,
package, release, or publicly share** any demo, lab, dev, or GA
artifact (including drops to partners such as Robert, customers, or
auditors), they must package the **right way** — best practices and
regulatory requirements, not shortcuts.

> **Interrupt for packaging agents:** Handing a partner an unsigned
> or unhashed build (no Authenticode/GPG path + no
> `release-manifests/<ver>/SHA256SUMS.txt`) **violates this hard
> rule**, even for a "quick demo," unless the operator gives an
> **explicit override in the same message**.

## The rule

> **Every publicly shared build is signed where the platform allows
> and always accompanied by a complete per-version SHA-256 manifest
> of all shipping components. Never share bits without both
> (signing + hashes) unless the operator overrides in the same
> message. Speak to customers and partners in simple US English —
> overcommunicate; minimize vernacular and IT jargon.**

## Required packaging path

| Step | Windows | Linux |
| ---- | ------- | ----- |
| Code / package signature | SSL.com eSigner Authenticode via `pim-build-tools/signing/Sign-WithSslComCodeSignTool.ps1` (and sibling `.sh` hosts) on every PE/MSI that ships | GPG package signing via `pim-build-tools/signing/sign-linux-packages-with-gpg.sh` (Authenticode does **not** apply to ELF/`.deb`/`.rpm`) |
| Orchestration | Prefer `Publish-SignedReleaseArtifacts.ps1` (`-SignSslCom`; GA: also `-SignLinuxPackages -RequireAllPresent`) | Same entrypoint / `publish-signed-release-artifacts.sh` |
| Component list | `pim-installers/scripts/ShippingComponentMatrix.ps1` | Same matrix |
| Hash manifest (required) | `pim-installers/release-manifests/<version>/SHA256SUMS.txt` + `COMPONENTS.md` (and `hashes.sha256` alias) | Same folder — Linux rows **must** appear |

MSI build scripts call `Write-ReleaseSha256Sums.ps1` after build; a
complete share still needs the full matrix (all products/platforms
in the drop) via the publish entrypoint.

## Forbidden

- Sharing unsigned Windows PE/MSI with partners or customers.
- Sharing Linux packages without the documented GPG path **and**
  hash-manifest rows when those packages are in the share set.
- Omitting `SHA256SUMS.txt` / `COMPONENTS.md` for "quick demos,"
  internal partner previews, or "we'll hash later."
- Signing only the outer MSI while leaving inner EXE/DLL unsigned.
- Inventing ad-hoc hash lists that skip
  `ShippingComponentMatrix.ps1` coverage.
- Partner-facing notes full of unexplained jargon; use plain words
  auditors and non-engineers can follow (US English).

## Override

Only the **operator**, in the **same message** as the share request,
may waive signing or the hash manifest (e.g. "share unsigned to
Robert, skip hashes"). Agents must not invent a waiver. Log the
override plainly when reporting what was shared.

## Communication (customers / partners)

- **Overcommunicate** process: what was signed, how to verify
  (`Get-FileHash` / `sha256sum -c`), which version folder holds
  `SHA256SUMS.txt` and `COMPONENTS.md`.
- **Simple US English** — short sentences; spell out terms once
  ("digital signature" before "Authenticode") when useful.
- Minimal vernacular / IT speak in partner-facing text. Source
  identifiers stay US English per
  [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §1.

## Rationale (regulatory)

Maps to **NIST SP 800-53 SI-7** (software, firmware, and information
integrity) and CMMC expectations for signed code **or** documented
acceptable hashes per version — this product line does **both**. See
[`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §3 (SI family)
and §6 (commit / audit hygiene). Branding and legal payloads in
packages still come only from
[`branding-assets.mdc`](branding-assets.mdc) / `core-assets`.

